{"kind":"Notebook","sha256":"4854a47db975485f63468c4eabc01912d62935998ef418861c6d45325f51ba58","slug":"localgroupcartography","location":"/01_Lectures/Week 6/LocalGroupCartography.ipynb","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Hands-on Practice: Local Group Cartography","content_includes_title":false,"kernelspec":{"name":"ugali_kernel","display_name":"ugali_kernel","language":"python"},"github":"https://astro-rps.github.io/","keywords":[],"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"edit_url":"https://astro-rps.github.io//blob/main/01_Lectures/Week 6/LocalGroupCartography.ipynb","exports":[{"format":"ipynb","filename":"LocalGroupCartography.ipynb","url":"/build/LocalGroupCartograph-4a5d057e198755510cb0db636d473316.ipynb"}]},"widgets":{},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","data":{},"children":[{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Astro-RPS Week 6 Saturday Bootcamp","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"epYNW4EESX"}],"identifier":"astro-rps-week-6-saturday-bootcamp","label":"Astro-RPS Week 6 Saturday Bootcamp","html_id":"astro-rps-week-6-saturday-bootcamp","implicit":true,"key":"usrD3SZfiH"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mapping the Local Group of Galaxies","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"r6avRlGX5M"}],"identifier":"mapping-the-local-group-of-galaxies","label":"Mapping the Local Group of Galaxies","html_id":"mapping-the-local-group-of-galaxies","implicit":true,"key":"B2DDsoWjHj"}],"key":"vRDcJD0yZC"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","data":{},"children":[{"type":"heading","depth":4,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Context","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"uDEwQ39q01"}],"identifier":"context","label":"Context","html_id":"context","implicit":true,"key":"gXIZJQyNnX"}],"key":"ElJhWToFc5"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","data":{},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this practice, we are going to make a map of the Local Group of galaxies (and associated star clusters). Roughly speaking, the Local Group refers to the gravitationally-bound group of galaxies within which the Milky Way resides; it spans out to roughly 10 million light years from the Milky Way (3 Megaparsecs, in more common units in the field). This volume contains just two massive galaxies -- the Milky Way and Andromeda -- in addition to hundreds of low-mass dwarf galaxies and thousands of star clusters.","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"rE6xl9NAPq"}],"key":"MqvIjXtcBm"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We usually think of the positions of things in the Local Group in Galactocentric Cartesian coordinates (X, Y, Z), where the origin is at the center of the Milky Way galaxy. However, we cannot observe these directly. Instead, we typically record the angular positions of stars/galaxies, etc., in “Right Ascension” (0-360 degrees) and “Declination” (-90 to 90 degrees) as well as their distances with respect to the Sun (their “heliocentric distances”). These measurable quantities can be converted to the Galactocentric coordinate system, assuming a reference frame for the Milky Way.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"zZkEQ3lB5L"}],"key":"FCV2fTmGvv"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"For today’s exercise, we’re going to give you this conversion in the form of a function below.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"yucbPrMduK"}],"key":"rgOuOoRwJB"}],"key":"ZHY2WLQvxy"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","data":{},"children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"from astropy import units as u\nfrom astropy.coordinates import SkyCoord, Galactocentric\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef convert_to_galactocentric(ra, dec, distance_kpc):\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert RA, Dec, and heliocentric distance to Galactocentric Coordinates.\n    \n    Parameters:\n        ra (float): Right Ascension in degrees\n        dec (float): Declination in degrees\n        distance_kpc (float): Heliocentric distance in kpc\n        \n    Returns:\n        tuple: (GCX, GCY, GCZ) in kpc\n    \"\"\"\n    # Create a SkyCoord object\n    coord = SkyCoord(ra=ra * u.deg, dec=dec * u.deg, distance=distance_kpc * u.kpc, frame='icrs')\n    \n    # Convert to Galactocentric coordinates\n    gc_coord = coord.transform_to(Galactocentric())\n    \n    # Convert to Cartesian coordinates\n    gcx = gc_coord.cartesian.x.to(u.kpc).value\n    gcy = gc_coord.cartesian.y.to(u.kpc).value\n    gcz = gc_coord.cartesian.z.to(u.kpc).value\n    \n    return gcx, gcy, gcz\n","key":"yPIbZLMZXo"},{"type":"output","id":"fjGG9h1cTCaqIwPm6zQzM","data":[],"key":"lAZ7xdbQGQ"}],"key":"htVjpo5qHR"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","data":{},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"An example of using this function for a single trio of (RA,DEC,distance):","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"ETy9WYNHfc"}],"key":"KPP6xd1ael"}],"key":"liyWaGEd2m"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","data":{},"children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# Example usage:\nra = 180.0  # degrees\ndec = 45.0  # degrees\ndistance = 10.0  # kpc\nx, y, z = convert_to_galactocentric(ra, dec, distance)\nprint(f\"X: {sgx:.2f} kpc\")\nprint(f\"Y: {sgy:.2f} kpc\")\nprint(f\"Z: {sgz:.2f} kpc\")","key":"k4PrEMXm6i"},{"type":"output","id":"pfMLYjmhHgZ_RLZQh-9T6","data":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"X: -11.13 kpc\nY: 1.79 kpc\nZ: 9.39 kpc\n"}],"key":"cKcDX1qRvM"}],"key":"Ymv291nt1c"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","data":{},"children":[{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exercise Part 1: Assembling a Database of Galactocentric Cartesian Coordinates for Local Group Galaxies","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"YpBQmGTt7W"}],"key":"zNjjkZTnj3"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this exercise, we will use the “Local Volume Database” published by Pace (2024), available through a GitHub repository (","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"JvhW1qKbpt"},{"type":"link","url":"https://github.com/apace7/local_volume_database","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"https://​github​.com​/apace7​/local​_volume​_database","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"fH8tUi17GT"}],"urlSource":"https://github.com/apace7/local_volume_database","error":true,"key":"m0hIVAwrlL"},{"type":"text","value":").","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"pKk9bOeuMh"}],"key":"u9v6RSF1r5"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Navigate to the link above and locate the “Releases” section on the right-hand side. Click on the latest release and 